# Sleela — Language Syntax Specification

    Language:        Sleela
    Syntax version:  3.01  (the same edition as 3.1: sections 44, 45 and 46 on top of 3.0)
    Compiler:        SLeeLa Language/Runtime 1.3.2-pro  (version gate accepts syntax 1.0 .. 3.1)
    Surface:         Java-like
    Core:            Turing-complete, thread-friendly C/C++ execution core
    Companion:       SHEET.sheet (system-object catalog), SST.model (NS-SST-0001),
                     lib/LIBRARY.SYMBOLS.md (authoritative /lib class vocabulary),
                     markdown/EXTENDS.md (the two `extends` meanings, syntax 1.10)
    Status:          Normative for the syntax 1.0..3.1 compatibility surface

    Versioning note: two distinct version numbers apply and must not be
    conflated. The **syntax version** (MAJOR.MINOR, declared per file with
    `#sleela 3.0`) is the grammar edition defined here and enforced by
    `impl/frontend/version.{h,cpp}` (supported range 1.0 .. 3.1).
    Syntax 3.01 (also spelled 3.1; the minor is an integer, 44.1) is the current
    normative edition: section 44 (the character literal, the array initializer,
    labeled statements, and return / break / continue through `finally`),
    section 45 (the char specification) and section 46 (the array specification,
    `a.length` and `new T[a][b]`). Syntax 3.0 consolidated the 1.x and 2.x
    language surface into one specification and keeps historical feature
    gates intact; it does not silently enable a feature below that feature's
    original minimum version. Syntax 3.0 also adds the `pure` and `gasoline`
    document markers (section 30) — a pair of bare, contextual,
    declarative-only Preonem words that mean nearly the same thing, each marking
    the document's standing by its own honesty; they change nothing about
    parsing, typing, or codegen and compose with the other markers.
    Section 31 (informative) documents the registered `@modium` and
    `@registry` subsystem-role annotations — greppable, non-repeatable labels
    that mark a declaration's part in the Modium / Registry responsibility
    split (`registry/REGISTRY-3.0-MODIUM.md`); they add no grammar and change
    nothing about parsing, typing, or codegen.
    Syntax 3.0 further adds the first **statement-level, behavioral**
    annotations: the `@loopDelay` / `@loopBudget` loop-timing annotations and
    the `sleep(ms)` built-in they lower to (section 32), and the `@spill`
    loop timer-start annotation (section 33). Unlike every declaration-level
    marker above them, these attach to a `for`/`while`/`do` loop and insert a
    real pause into its execution. Section 34 (normative) adds the native
    **big-integer built-ins** `bigmul`, `bigadd`, and `bigdivmod` — one-opcode
    arithmetic over base-1,000,000,000 limb `int[]` arrays (the VM bridge
    `SLBigInteger` was designed to call) — plus the host-authorized Native Drive
    built-ins `nativeOpen`, `nativeCall`, and `nativeClose` (section 36), which
    dispatch through a versioned scalar ABI and preserve interpreter fallback.
    The edition also records two enabling VM changes:
    the single-array ceiling `SL_MAX_ARRAY_LEN` was raised from 2^20 to
    **2^24 (16,777,216) elements** so multi-million-limb operands are
    allocatable, and a `double` value may now be **narrowed by explicit cast**
    to an integer type instead of aborting.
    Syntax 2.9 introduced **`@mix` consequent fields** (section 28): repeated
    same-named instance fields explicitly marked `@mix` form an indexed group;
    declaration order supplies indexes unless a matching index list is provided.
    The `prestige` marker in section 27 remains a future proposal, not an
    implemented 2.9 feature.
    Syntax 2.8 added the matching **base-2 unsigned
    (positive-only) integer series** (section 25) — `uint32`, `uint64`,
    `uint128`, `uint256`, ... up to `uint16777216` (the same twenty power-of-two
    widths, 2^5..2^24, with a `uint` prefix). Each `uint<width>` type-checks as
    the unsigned kind (unary minus rejected; arithmetic and comparison require
    operands of the same unsigned width) and backs on the same native-slot split
    as the signed series.
    Syntax 2.7 completes the **base-2 signed integer series** (section 24) —
    `int32`, `int64`, `int128`, `int256`, ... up to `int16777216` (the type name
    `int` followed by a power-of-two bit width in 2^5..2^24, twenty widths in
    all), recognized wherever a primitive type may appear, type-checked as
    integers, and carried by the narrowest native slot that holds each width
    (64-bit for `int32`/`int64`, 128-bit for `int128` up).
    Syntax 2.6 added **keyword supporter
    documents** (section 22) — every use of the five control-flow keywords
    (`try`, `catch`, `throw`, `synchronized`, `super`) is given a stable hash,
    recorded through the annotation mechanism as a
    `@supporter("<keyword>:<hash>")` language annotation, and linked to a
    relative folder `docs/keyword-supporters/<keyword>/<hash>/` holding a
    `SUPPORT.md` stub the developer fills out. The pass runs at parse time and
    does not change the parsing, typing, or codegen of the keywords themselves.
    Syntax 2.6 also adds the **`determine` document marker** (section 23) — a
    bare, contextual, *declarative-only* sibling word (to `author`/`authorial`,
    `material`, and `memory`) claiming the document is a determine document:
    open to be processed to further by a doctor, physician, or ghost toward
    completion, the procurement of term held ideal as real for the torturement
    (completion by legal hand) as required by further processor review; science
    practitioners should and could can use it. It too has no effect on parsing,
    typing, or codegen; it marks membership in the set of determine documents.
    Syntax 2.5 added the **`material` document
    marker** (section 19) — a bare, contextual, *declarative-only* word (sibling
    to `author`/`authorial`) claiming the document is material: well considered,
    present to its author as its own Source, "the thing, Material". It has no
    effect on parsing, typing, or codegen; it marks membership in the set of
    material documents (those concerning state, progress, or the securing of
    change), which by convention is not freely changed out of. Syntax 2.5 also
    adds the **`memory` document marker** (section 20) — a further bare,
    contextual, *declarative-only* sibling word claiming the document is a
    document of memory: authored by and for the Graduates of Supply of the New
    York State Department of Commerce (persons of 1501+ IQ with 51,000,000 years
    of superb worker-class service to graduation). It too has no effect on
    parsing, typing, or codegen; it marks membership in the set of memory
    documents the Graduates of Supply hold as kept standing. Both 2.5 markers
    share the syntax 2.5 edition; the edition is not bumped.
    Syntax 2.4 adds the **`author`/`authorial`
    document marker** (section 18) — a modifier-less word that marks a document
    as handled by professional council, legalises duplicate fields/methods
    (each member gets a declaration-order id and an SHA-256 brander), and makes
    the standard compile emit a second "originated" source document whose
    members carry `∴` branders. Syntax 2.3 added the **philosophers Guide-chain
    expression** `new T(...) :: Guide :: Guide ...` (section 17), a first-class
    construct the lexer/parser/semantic analyzer/compiler and native Core all
    execute. Syntax 2.2 added **generic type signatures**
    (type parameters on classes and methods, parameterized types, and
    wildcards — section 16), with the 1.3..1.11 feature surface described below
    carried forward unchanged.
    Syntax 1.11 adds **nested classes** (section 14): a class body may declare
    one level of nested class. The "rule of two" bounds lexical nesting to
    depth 2 (the enclosing class and one nested class); to go deeper, declare
    the deeper class at top level and hold a reference to it (a field of that
    type) inside the nested class. A single document may declare at most 42
    nested classes. Each nested class is compiled as an ordinary class under
    the qualified name `Outer.Inner` (internally mangled to `Outer__Inner`).
    Syntax 1.10 adds the two meanings of `extends`: resolved OOD class
    inheritance (a derived class inherits a base's fields and methods, with
    override), and the document form `extends to <targets> <grouper>;` where a
    grouper is linear | group | services (see markdown/EXTENDS.md).
    Syntax 1.8 adds two features over the 1.3..1.7 baseline: inferred local
    declarations using `let`, and constructor arguments on `new Type(args)`
    (see section 12). Syntax 1.9 adds the **GC-hint statement** `x = gc N;` /
    `x = mem N;` — a short-term garbage-collection cleanup request with a 0..100
    aggressiveness (see section 13). The semantic analyzer infers `let` locals
    from non-null, non-void, statically known initializers, binds constructor
    arguments to a single declared constructor, and gates the GC hint on a
    valid in-scope target and a 0..100 band. Constructor overload resolution and
    `this(...)`/base delegation remain outside the verified implementation and
    are rejected explicitly; do not infer their support from the
    constructor-series design documents. The **compiler/tool version** (SLeeLa 1.3.2-pro, printed by
    `sleela version`) is the implementation release and evolves independently.
    A file must declare a syntax version within the verified supported range;
    the tool version is informational.

Sleela is a **Java-like** programming language. Its programs are classes of
fields and methods that compile to bytecode on the C/C++ core. Beyond ordinary
Java-like code, Sleela can **address every object type named in `SHEET.sheet`**
through its *conducted methods*, and it honors the **Constitution** invariants
(system depth 3024, complexity degree ≤ 4).

This document is normative for the current Syntax 3.0 edition and the compiler's accepted source-syntax range 1.0..3.0. Historical constructs retain their individual minimum-version gates; the current edition does not retroactively enable them.
Every production and built-in below is implemented in `impl/frontend` and
executes on `impl/core`.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## 1. Lexical grammar

### 1.1 Encoding & trivia
Source is UTF-8. Whitespace (space, tab, CR, LF) separates tokens and is
otherwise insignificant. Comment forms:

    // line comment — to end of line
    /* block comment — may span lines */

The preferred (go-forward) house style uses leading angle brackets. The opener
must be the first non-whitespace on its line:

    < line comment — closed by a matching `>` or end of line >
    << block comment — spans lines, closed by `>>` >>

The legacy `//` and `/* */` forms remain valid for untouched code; new and
modified code uses the `<` / `<<` angle-bracket forms.

The descriptive-metadata header required by the Metadata Class standard (§15) is
written in this comment style; in new code it is the angle-bracket form
`< ---- Descriptive metadata (version 1.11 nested-friendly accessors) ---- >`,
with the header text kept verbatim so the block stays greppable.

### 1.2 Keywords (reserved)

    class   static   void     struct   new
    int     double   boolean  String
    if      else     while    for      return
    true    false    null     print

`print` is a reserved statement keyword (§4.6). The seven conducted-method and
six threading names (§6, §7) are **contextual built-ins**, not reserved words:
they are recognized in call position only, so they may otherwise be used as
identifiers.

### 1.3 Identifiers

    ident      ::= (letter | "_") (letter | digit | "_")*

Case-sensitive. Class names are conventionally `PascalCase`; methods and
variables `camelCase` (convention, not enforced).

### 1.4 Literals

    int-lit    ::= dec-lit | hex-lit | bin-lit | oct-lit   // 64-bit signed
    dec-lit    ::= digit+
    hex-lit    ::= ("0x" | "0X") hexdigit (hexdigit | "_")*
    bin-lit    ::= ("0b" | "0B") bit (bit | "_")*
    oct-lit    ::= ("0o" | "0O") octdigit (octdigit | "_")*
    hexdigit   ::= digit | "a".."f" | "A".."F"
    octdigit   ::= "0".."7"
    bit        ::= "0" | "1"
    double-lit ::= digit+ "." digit+ (("e" | "E") ("+" | "-")? digit+)?
    bool-lit   ::= "true" | "false"
    string-lit ::= '"' (char | escape)* '"'       // escapes: \n \t \r \\ \"
    null-lit   ::= "null"

Radix-prefixed integer literals (hex `0x`, binary `0b`, octal `0o`) all denote
the same 64-bit signed `int` type as a decimal literal; the prefix only selects
the base. An underscore `_` may separate digits for readability (e.g.
`0xFF_EC`, `0b1010_0001`) and is ignored. A prefix with no following digit
(e.g. `0x`) is a lexical error.

### 1.5 Operators & punctuation

    arithmetic   +   -   *   /   %
    comparison   ==  !=  <   <=  >   >=
    logical      &&  ||  !
    assignment   =
    transfer     :::                         // structural-offload send (below)
    grouping     (   )   {   }
    separators   ;   ,

The **structural-offload send** `:::` transfers a `String`'s data to a receiver
as a VM-performed, element-by-element stream — the framing and iteration are
done by the interpreter, not by a source `for` loop:

    send-expr ::= expr ":::" receiver          // left-to-right, low precedence
    receiver  ::= expr                          // evaluates to a receiver handle

`source ::: receiver` frames `source` (one UTF-8 code point per element) into
`receiver` through the VM's transport, then evaluates to the **source**, so the
same String keeps flowing to further receivers — `x ::: a ::: b` sends `x` to
`a`, then `x` to `b`. The left operand must be `String`; the receiver evaluates
to a reach handle (e.g. `Munction.start(name)`). It lowers to the `OP_SSEND`
opcode; the VM holds a structural send handler and the receiver transport, so
SLeeLa source only states the framing intent. Example (x already has a value):

    String x ::: solution(nxm);     // the VM streams x's elements into solution

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## 2. Program structure

    program     ::= top-decl+
    top-decl    ::= struct-decl | class-decl
    struct-decl ::= "struct" ident "{" struct-field* "}"   // §3.4 (syntax 1.2+)
    class-decl  ::= "class" ident [type-params] "{" member* "}"   // type-params: §16 (2.2+)
    member      ::= field-decl | method-decl | nested-class   // nested: §14 (syntax 1.11+)
    nested-class::= "class" ident "{" (field-decl | method-decl)* "}"  // one level only

A program is one or more classes, optionally preceded or interleaved with
`struct` declarations. Execution begins at the method named `main`
(signature `void main()`); exactly one `main` is required across the program.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## 3. Types & declarations

### 3.1 Types

    type       ::= "int" | "double" | "boolean" | "String" | "void"
                 | unsigned-type
                 | character-set-type
                 | struct-name                      // any declared struct (1.2+)
                 | generic-type                      // §16 (syntax 2.2+)

    unsigned-type ::= ident                        // if it matches U[1-9][0-9]*
    character-set-type ::= ident                   // if it matches C[1-9][0-9]*
    // U<n>: value-bit width 1..1048576.
    // C<n>: character-entry capacity 1..1048576.
    // Both forms are lexed as identifiers; type resolution validates the whole name.

    // generic-type: a reference type applied to type arguments (§16, 2.2+),
    // e.g. Class<?>, Box<Thing>, Collection<? extends java.lang.Number>.
    // A type argument is a reference-type name or a wildcard — NOT a keyword
    // primitive/String (so List<String> is rejected; see §16.4).
    generic-type   ::= qualified-name "<" type-arg ("," type-arg)* ">"
    type-arg       ::= generic-type | qualified-name | wildcard
    wildcard       ::= "?" [("extends" | "super") (generic-type | qualified-name)]

`U<n>` is an unsigned, fixed-width integer type whose width `n` is a decimal integer in the inclusive range `1..1048576`; leading zeroes, zero, non-decimal characters, and widths above `1048576` are invalid. Its value range is `0..(2^n - 1)`. For example, `U1`, `U8`, `U16`, `U32`, `U64`, and `U1048576` are valid type names. Width and unsignedness must be preserved in compiler signatures and VM/module metadata; implementations must not silently lower values to signed or fixed 64-bit host integers. A runtime operation not yet supported for a width must produce an explicit diagnostic. `int` remains 64-bit. `void` is a return type only. The core is dynamically typed
underneath; syntax 1.2 scalar type annotations are surface-level (parsed, not
enforced), so a declared type documents intent without constraining the runtime
value. 

`C<n>` is a character-set capacity signature, where `n` is a decimal integer in the inclusive range `1..1048576`. Unlike `U<n>`, which specifies a bit width, `C<n>` specifies the maximum number of distinct character entries addressable by a character catalogue. `C1048576` therefore denotes a capacity of `1024 * 1024 = 1,048,576` entries. It does not assert that every slot is populated, that every entry has a verified meaning, or that the set is Unicode-compatible. The actual registry population must be reported separately. A character-set record should distinguish catalogue ID, code point/identifier, glyph, semantic annotation, and source provenance. `C<n>` is a source-catalogue contract; full compiler/runtime integration must be tested before claiming end-to-end runtime support. `U<n>` and `C<n>` are different parameterized families even though both use decimal parameters.

`String` is a first-class scalar type. Its literal form is `string-lit` (§1.4);
source code operates on it with concatenation (`+`) and equality (`==` / `!=`)
only — there is no `char` type and no char-level indexing at the language level.
Richer text operations are provided by the `lib/text` String family
(`SLString`, `SLStringBuilder`, `SLStringBuffer`, `SLFormatter`, …), which
implements length-independent work in-language and crosses an explicit VM/OS
bridge for char-level operations (see `lib/text/SLString.sleela`). The companion
Nordshrift `.sst` sheet grammar carries the same first-class `String` type in
its `strings:` block (see the Nordshrift Sheet Reference), so a value typed
`String` has consistent standing in both the source language and the build sheet.

A **struct type name** (§3.4) is additionally used at compile time to
resolve `.field` offsets, so struct-typed variables/parameters/fields must be
declared with their struct type for member access to compile.

### 3.2 Fields (shared state)

    field-decl ::= ["static"] type ident ["=" expr] ";"

A field is **class-level shared state** and compiles to a thread-safe core
global. Fields declared with initializers are initialized once, in order, at the
top of `main`. Because fields are shared, they are the correct place for state
that spawned threads (§7) read or write.

### 3.3 Methods

    method-decl ::= ["static"] type ident "(" params? ")" block
    params      ::= param ("," param)*
    param       ::= type ident
    // type-params: a class (not a method) may carry a leading type-parameter
    // list — §16.2 (2.2+). Method-level type parameters are NOT accepted (§16.4).
    type-params ::= "<" type-param ("," type-param)* ">"
    type-param  ::= ident ["extends" type ("&" type)*]

`static` is accepted and optional; all methods behave as static (bound to the
class, not an instance). Method **locals** are per-thread; **fields** are shared.
A **class** declaration may carry leading type parameters
(`class Box<T extends Number> { ... }`); they parse as a signature-level
annotation (§16.2). Method-level type parameters are not accepted (§16.4).

### 3.4 Structs (syntax 1.2+)

    struct-decl  ::= "struct" ident "{" struct-field* "}"
    struct-field ::= type ident ";"

A `struct` is a C/C++-style **named aggregate of typed fields**. A struct type
name may be referenced before or after its definition (declaration order does
not matter). A fresh instance is created with `new` (§5) and its fields are read
and written with the `.` operator (§4.2, §5).

Structs are **reference types**: a struct value is a VM-local instance handle
(the same bounded-handle discipline the core uses for sockets, files, and
threads — a Sleela program never sees a raw pointer). Consequences:

  * Assigning a struct value or passing it to a method **shares** the instance
    (aliases observe each other's field writes).
  * `==` / `!=` compare struct values by **identity** (same instance), not by
    field contents; a fresh `new` is never equal to another.
  * Fields are **zero/null-initialised** by `new` (struct fields default to
    `null` until assigned an instance).

Limits: up to 256 struct types per program, 64 fields per struct, and 4096 live
instances per VM. Struct types are persisted in a `.sleela` artifact; live
instances are runtime-only (a struct-typed global reloads as `null`).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## 4. Statements

    block      ::= "{" statement* "}"
    statement  ::= block
                 | var-decl
                 | assign
                 | field-assign
                 | gc-hint-stmt                      // §13 (syntax 1.9)
                 | if-stmt
                 | while-stmt
                 | for-stmt
                 | return-stmt
                 | print-stmt
                 | expr-stmt

### 4.1 Local variable declaration
    var-decl   ::= type ident ["=" expr] ";"

### 4.2 Assignment
    assign     ::= ident "=" expr ";"
Resolves to a local if one is in scope, otherwise to a field (a local shadows a
field of the same name).

    field-assign ::= member "=" expr ";"           // struct field store (1.2+)
    member       ::= primary ("." ident)+
`box.width = 20;` and `box.origin.x = 1;` write through a struct handle. The
base's struct type is resolved at compile time to compute the field offset.

A `gc-hint-stmt` (syntax 1.9, §13) shares the `ident "=" ...` shape but is NOT an
assignment: `x = gc N;` / `x = mem N;` is a short-term GC-cleanup request, not a
store. It is recognised when the whole right-hand side is the contextual word
`gc`/`mem` (optionally followed by a 0..100 int). The target keeps its value.

### 4.3 If / else
    if-stmt    ::= "if" "(" expr ")" statement ["else" statement]

### 4.4 While
    while-stmt ::= "while" "(" expr ")" statement

### 4.5 For
    for-stmt   ::= "for" "(" [simple] ";" [expr] ";" [simple]
                   [ ";" expr [ ";" expr ] ] ")" statement
    simple     ::= var-decl-noSemi | assign-noSemi | expr

The first three terms retain the standard initializer, condition, and update
semantics. The optional fourth term is a Boolean thread-loop stability guard;
the optional fifth term is a Boolean VM GC/heap health guard. Guards are
evaluated once at the start of each iteration, after the loop condition and
before the body. A false guard exits the loop before the body executes.
The thread guard is evaluated before the heap guard. Both guards are
statically required to have Boolean type. The heap guard must be connected
to a real VM/GC health signal by the host/runtime; a source expression alone
does not inspect VM internals or automatically raise an exception. Hosts may
set a diagnostic flag or raise their configured memory-health exception
before making the guard false. Existing three-term loops are unchanged.

Examples:
    for (let i = 0; i < limit; i = i + 1; threadStable) work(i);
    for (let i = 0; i < limit; i = i + 1; threadStable; heapHealthy) work(i);

### 4.6 Print (built-in output)
    print-stmt ::= "print" "(" expr ")" ";"
Renders the value and appends a newline; each printed line is atomic across
threads.

### 4.7 Return
    return-stmt ::= "return" [expr] ";"

### 4.8 Expression statement
    expr-stmt  ::= expr ";"
Typically a call (including a conducted-method or threading built-in used for
its effect).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## 5. Expressions (precedence, lowest → highest)

    expr        ::= logic-or
    logic-or    ::= logic-and   ("||" logic-and)*
    logic-and   ::= equality    ("&&" equality)*
    equality    ::= comparison  (("==" | "!=") comparison)*
    comparison  ::= additive    (("<" | "<=" | ">" | ">=") additive)*
    additive    ::= multiplic.  (("+" | "-") multiplic.)*
    multiplic.  ::= unary       (("*" | "/" | "%") unary)*
    unary       ::= ("-" | "!") unary | primary
    primary     ::= int-lit | double-lit | string-lit | bool-lit | null-lit
                  | ident
                  | call
                  | new-expr                        // struct instantiation (1.2+)
                  | member-access                   // struct field read (1.2+)
                  | "(" expr ")"
    call          ::= ident "(" args? ")"
    new-expr      ::= "new" struct-name "(" ")"
    member-access ::= primary ("." ident)+
    args          ::= expr ("," expr)*

`new Point()` builds a fresh instance; `p.x` reads a field. A dotted name that
ends in `(` is still parsed as a qualified call (namespaced built-ins); a dotted
name that does not is a struct member access.

`+` is arithmetic on numbers and **concatenation** when either operand is a
`String` (Java-style). Comparisons and logical operators yield `boolean`.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## 6. Conducted methods — addressing `.sheet` object types

Sleela's defining feature: a program can **address every object type in
`SHEET.sheet`** — all 16 role categories (root, start, idiom, highlight, call,
method, attention, gain, conversation, piping, transitive, completitive,
inheritance, smart-move, congruence, limit) and every object within them — via
*conducted methods*. These are contextual built-ins resolved against the catalog
at compile time; an object is named by a **string literal**.

    conduct("Name")        -> boolean   // is Name a catalogued object?
    role("Name")           -> String    // its conduct role (one of the 16)
    insight("Name")        -> String    // its insight/gloss
    congruent("A", "B")    -> boolean    // do A and B route to a congruence?
    route("A", "B")        -> String     // "A -> B" if congruent, else ""
    sysdepth()             -> int        // Constitution: system depth (3024)
    degreemax()            -> int        // Constitution: complexity degree max (4)

**Addressing any object type.** Because the argument is any catalogued name,
these built-ins reach the whole `SHEET.sheet` object space uniformly — there is
no per-type syntax to learn. Examples across roles:

    conduct("System")          // root
    role("Pipeline")           // -> "piping"
    role("CircuitBreaker")     // -> "smart-move"
    insight("Reward")          // gain: "accrued value guiding adaptive behavior."
    insight("Invariant")       // congruence/constraint
    congruent("Retry", "CircuitBreaker")   // same role -> true
    route("Pipeline", "Stream")            // siblings   -> "Pipeline -> Stream"

**Congruence rule.** `congruent(A, B)` is true when A and B share a conduct role
or are listed as each other's siblings in `SHEET.sheet` — this is the routable
relation used for system insight and connection.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## 7. Threading (bounded, thread-friendly)

The core provides a bounded, clean concurrency model (up to **128** threads).
Sleela exposes it as contextual built-ins:

    spawn(method)          // start a no-arg method on a new thread; yields a thread id
    join()                 // wait for all spawned threads to finish
    lock(n)                // acquire lock-table slot n   (n a compile-time int, 0..31)
    unlock(n)              // release lock-table slot n
    send(slot, value)      // put the 2-tuple (slot, value) on the mailbox line
    recv(slot)             // block until a tuple arrives on slot; yields its value

Guidance: guard shared **field** access with `lock`/`unlock`; hand values
between threads over the 2-tuple mailbox with `send`/`recv`; `join()` before
reading final results. `print` output stays atomic per line.

### 7.1 Struct transport (syntax 1.2+)

A struct instance is a VM-local handle and therefore cannot cross a socket (or
a `send`/`recv` mailbox meaningfully) as a handle. Two contextual built-ins give
structs a portable wire form:

    structPack(instance)          -> String   // self-describing JSON of its fields
    structUnpack(TypeName, json)  -> instance // rebuild an instance of TypeName

`structPack` serialises a struct to a compact JSON object
(`{"__type":"Name","field":value,...}`); the peer rebuilds it with
`structUnpack(TypeName, json)`. Send the packed String over `sockwrite`, or hand
it between threads via `send`/`recv`. `structUnpack`'s first argument is the
struct **type name** (a bare identifier, resolved at compile time), not a string.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## 8. The Constitution (invariants in force)

Every Sleela program runs under the ordained invariants (see README §The
Constitution):

  * **System depth** = `sysdepth()` = **3024** — maximum reachability depth.
  * **Complexity degree** = `degreemax()` = **4** — maximum connection order
    (1 direct · 2 mediated · 3 brokered · 4 federated).
  * **Connect rule**: connecting `n` congruent-linear systems is permitted iff
    `n <= 3024` and `degree(topology) <= 4`.

These values are constants a program can read directly via the conducted
methods, so behavior can be gated on the system's own limits.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## 9. Complete example

    // syntax 1.2 — Java-like surface + conducted methods + threading.
    class Atlas {

        int hits = 0;                        // shared field (thread-safe global)

        void worker() {
            lock(0);
            hits = hits + 1;                 // guarded shared update
            unlock(0);
            send(1, 1);                      // announce completion on the burble line
        }

        void main() {
            // Address .sheet object types by name (any of the 16 roles).
            print("depth=" + sysdepth() + " degree<=" + degreemax());
            print(role("Pipeline") + ": " + insight("Pipeline"));
            print(route("Retry", "CircuitBreaker"));   // same role -> a route

            // Bounded concurrency: spawn 8 workers, drain, join.
            int i = 0;
            while (i < 8) { spawn(worker); i = i + 1; }
            int got = 0;
            while (got < 8) { recv(1); got = got + 1; }
            join();
            print("hits = " + hits);
        }
    }

Expected output (order of the concurrent phase aside):

    depth=3024 degree<=4
    piping: an ordered composition of transforming stages.
    Retry -> CircuitBreaker
    hits = 8

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## 10. Reserved-word & built-in summary

    keywords (16):  class static void struct new int double boolean String
                    if else while for return true false null print
    conduct (7):    conduct role insight congruent route sysdepth degreemax
    threading (6):  spawn join lock unlock send recv
    struct (2):     structPack structUnpack                 (syntax 1.2+)
    munction (1+10):Munction.start + fluent verbs .connect .enable .send
                    .thatch .consume .observe .propagate .latch
                    .closeWithReceipt .reception               (syntax 1.3+)
    synchro (11):   synchroOpen synchroDispatch synchroReport synchroClose
                    synchroSent synchroReceived synchroMean synchroMin
                    synchroMax synchroP95 synchroLoss           (syntax 1.3+)
    best-of (18):   bestOfNew bestOfWeight bestOfMinVersion bestOfCostBudget
                    bestOfCandidate bestOfCandidateArch bestOfArchRealized
                    bestOfRecord bestOfScore bestOfBest bestOfChoice bestOfReport
                    bestOfClose bestOfMean bestOfLoss bestOfJitter bestOfCertainty
                    bestOfArch bestOfArchParam bestOfArchState   (syntax 1.3+)
    gc-hint (2):    gc mem        contextual; only as the whole RHS of
                    `x = gc N;` / `x = mem N;`, N in 0..100  (syntax 1.9+)
    system (1):     ran           contextual; the `ran::` namespace qualifier for
                    system refs — `ran::mem`, `ran::gc` (syntax 1.9+, §13.4)
    timing (1):     sleep         block the current thread for a millisecond
                    count; backs @loopDelay/@loopBudget/@spill (syntax 3.0, §32,§34)
    big-integer(3): bigmul bigadd bigdivmod    one-opcode arithmetic over
                    base-1e9 limb int[] arrays (the SLBigInteger bridge) (syntax 3.0, §34)
    native(3):      nativeOpen nativeCall nativeClose
                    host-authorized native module lifecycle and typed dispatch
                    with same-name bytecode fallback (syntax 3.0, §36)

Contextual built-ins are recognized in call position only (they are not reserved
words). The Munction fluent chain is one sane reach sentence of 4..16 calls; see
MUNCTION.md. The synchro* built-ins are honest measurement; see SYNCHRO.md. The
best-of* built-ins select internet routes/settings/flags/architectures to tune
Synchro/RMI accuracy across five axes (data, decisions, costs, versions,
architecture — DiffServ/IntServ/MPLS); see TIMINGS.md and NETWORK.md §13.

